<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">armor</ansi>

<ansi fg="command">Armor</ansi> takes a share of each blow that gets past your block, parry, or
dodge (<ansi fg="command">help defense</ansi>).

Each piece you wear adds its armor to your total, a shield's own armor
included. Each blow that lands is reduced by a random amount up to your
total, as a percentage, and the hit's line tells you how much was stopped:

  <ansi fg="command">(5 damage, 2 absorbed)</ansi>   your armor absorbed 2 of the blow

A shield adds nothing beyond its own armor: it earns its place by blocking.
Armor broken by a critical hit gives half its protection for the rest of
the fight (<ansi fg="command">help statuses</ansi>).

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Bulk</ansi>

Every piece of armor is <ansi fg="yellow">light</ansi>, <ansi fg="yellow">medium</ansi> or <ansi fg="yellow">heavy</ansi>, and your bulk is the
heaviest piece you wear. Toughness costs quickness, however strong you are:

  <ansi fg="yellow">Light</ansi>    no cost (cloth, a cape, a buckler)
  <ansi fg="yellow">Medium</ansi>   {{ configpct "GamePlay.Combat.BulkTempoMedium" }}% fewer turns, {{ configpct "GamePlay.Combat.BulkDodgeMedium" }}% less dodge (a leather vest or robe, chain, a shield)
  <ansi fg="yellow">Heavy</ansi>    {{ configpct "GamePlay.Combat.BulkTempoHeavy" }}% fewer turns, {{ configpct "GamePlay.Combat.BulkDodgeHeavy" }}% less dodge (a breastplate, a tower shield)

Bulk never touches a parry or a block, so a heavy warrior defends with
sword and shield, not footwork. Bulk is not weight: a piece's weight
adds to your burden (<ansi fg="command">help burden</ansi>), and the bulk costs come on top.
<ansi fg="command">status</ansi> shows your bulk, and <ansi fg="command">company compare</ansi> a companion's.

Armor comes in four paths: cloth and leather (light), medium and heavy,
each in tiers that protect more at the same weight. The tiers and the
numbers are in <ansi fg="command">help equipmenttiers</ansi>.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Training</ansi>

Each class is trained for armor up to a bulk:

  <ansi fg="yellow">Warrior</ansi>                    heavy
  <ansi fg="yellow">Ranger</ansi>                     medium
  <ansi fg="yellow">Halberdier</ansi>                 medium
  <ansi fg="yellow">Samurai</ansi>                    medium
  <ansi fg="yellow">Rogue, Cleric, Wizard, Witch, Doll Master</ansi>      light

You may wear heavier armor, at a real cost: its turn and dodge costs are
doubled, you lose {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.UntrainedSkillLoss" }} Attack and {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.UntrainedSkillLoss" }} Evasion (<ansi fg="command">help evasion</ansi>), and every
spell takes {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.UntrainedChantRounds" }} more round to chant. <ansi fg="command">equip</ansi> warns you when a piece is heavier
than your training.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help equipmenttiers</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help defense</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help shields</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help tempo</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help narration</ansi>.
